WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.354 --> 00:00:04.104
(gentle wind chime ambiance)

00:00:06.015 --> 00:00:07.298
- [Joey] Playing Hamlet has helped me ask

00:00:07.298 --> 00:00:08.788
questions of myself that usually

00:00:08.788 --> 00:00:11.371
I would leave very deep inside.

00:00:13.209 --> 00:00:15.904
I never thought I'd do anything like this:

00:00:15.904 --> 00:00:19.571
"To be, or not to be—
That is the question."

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:24.070
(playful electronic music)

00:00:29.852 --> 00:00:31.598
- [Lesley] Put your hands on your belly,

00:00:31.598 --> 00:00:33.431
take a deep breath in.

00:00:33.431 --> 00:00:35.063
My name is Lesley Currier. 

00:00:35.063 --> 00:00:36.373
I'm the founding managing director of

00:00:36.373 --> 00:00:38.528
Marin Shakespeare Company.

00:00:38.528 --> 00:00:41.036
(actors vocalizing together)

00:00:41.036 --> 00:00:42.879
The methodology of our program is inspired

00:00:42.879 --> 00:00:47.832
by drama therapy. It creates
laughter in the room.

00:00:47.832 --> 00:00:49.982
And it breaks down barriers between races,

00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:51.399
and ages, and groups.

00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:53.998
- You didn't ask that question,

00:00:53.998 --> 00:00:55.809
I wouldn't say "for here lies the point".

00:00:55.809 --> 00:00:57.661
- It's very beautiful work,

00:00:57.661 --> 00:01:00.927
and we've seen a lot of transformation.

00:01:00.927 --> 00:01:02.927
Most of the men in the group don't have

00:01:02.927 --> 00:01:03.912
any theater experience,

00:01:03.912 --> 00:01:06.142
and haven't studied Shakespeare before.

00:01:06.142 --> 00:01:08.183
So when we start talking about the plays,

00:01:08.183 --> 00:01:11.058
and the characters, they're just bringing

00:01:11.058 --> 00:01:12.656
their own personal life experience

00:01:12.656 --> 00:01:14.323
to the conversation.

00:01:16.466 --> 00:01:18.813
- "I shall heal my broken wings!"

00:01:18.813 --> 00:01:19.658
- When I first came in, I thought

00:01:19.658 --> 00:01:21.198
I would never act again.

00:01:21.198 --> 00:01:25.281
I attended college back
in Nebraska in 1968, ’69.

00:01:26.364 --> 00:01:29.364
I was a co-major, theater and music.

00:01:30.273 --> 00:01:32.766
When I came into prison,

00:01:32.766 --> 00:01:35.622
there is this certain demeanor

00:01:35.622 --> 00:01:37.411
that you have to establish.

00:01:37.411 --> 00:01:39.860
So in essence, I'm acting
on a day to day basis.

00:01:39.860 --> 00:01:41.211
Just to survive.

00:01:41.211 --> 00:01:44.044
To be able to be up on that stage,

00:01:45.113 --> 00:01:48.917
letting the true Steve come through,

00:01:48.917 --> 00:01:51.834
but in the person of the character.

00:01:53.501 --> 00:01:55.344
That's the freedom I get.

00:01:55.344 --> 00:01:57.631
And that's what I hope comes
out when I do Polonius.

00:01:57.631 --> 00:02:00.527
(practicing)
"Pastoral, historical come--"

00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:03.577
- [Joey] This role is so challenging

00:02:03.577 --> 00:02:06.957
that it makes me go through
the entire gamut of emotions.

00:02:06.957 --> 00:02:08.952
He's so conflicted that,

00:02:08.952 --> 00:02:12.849
man, I must've pulled from
every facet of my life.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:14.738
I've been betrayed before,

00:02:14.738 --> 00:02:16.821
I've had my trust broken,

00:02:19.914 --> 00:02:22.449
I've done really horrible
things to people,

00:02:22.449 --> 00:02:25.518
you know, in a rage, like Hamlet's done.

00:02:25.518 --> 00:02:26.658
(acting)
"Well you may see the

00:02:26.658 --> 00:02:28.082
in-most part of you!"

00:02:28.082 --> 00:02:30.415
- "What will that do?
Thou will not murder me!"

00:02:30.415 --> 00:02:33.443
(screaming)
"Help! Help!"

00:02:33.443 --> 00:02:37.432
- Over the years I've
learned I have to give back.

00:02:37.432 --> 00:02:39.083
That I also have to own up

00:02:39.083 --> 00:02:42.166
and look at what I did realistically.

00:02:43.026 --> 00:02:45.692
I'm hoping through other things that I do,

00:02:45.692 --> 00:02:48.877
my acting, I can give
something back to society.

00:02:48.877 --> 00:02:50.794
- I've been able to use

00:02:52.694 --> 00:02:54.826
all of my past experience
and kinda channel it

00:02:54.826 --> 00:02:56.306
into this character.

00:02:56.306 --> 00:02:58.649
I hope that playing my role,

00:02:58.649 --> 00:03:01.793
helps the other inmates here feel like

00:03:01.793 --> 00:03:03.960
they're part of the story.

00:03:04.854 --> 00:03:06.252
This doesn't just help me escape,

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:08.502
this helps them escape, too.

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.627
It also helps, uh, me gain a lot of empathy

00:03:11.627 --> 00:03:14.061
for, you know, what other people are going through.

00:03:14.061 --> 00:03:17.726
It's tearing open old wounds
to try to embody this,

00:03:17.726 --> 00:03:19.378
to bring the character alive,

00:03:19.378 --> 00:03:21.608
and bring other people within the story.

00:03:21.608 --> 00:03:22.858
It's difficult.

00:03:24.938 --> 00:03:27.073
But it's also really cathartic.

00:03:27.073 --> 00:03:28.490
It helps… It helps me heal.

00:03:31.795 --> 00:03:34.397
(acting)
"In this cruel world,

00:03:34.397 --> 00:03:36.480
“draw thy breath and pain.

00:03:38.687 --> 00:03:40.104
“To tell my story.

00:03:41.303 --> 00:03:43.053
“The rest in silence."

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:48.817
(audience applause)

